|id=gow |toc=no
|text=
* '''1615''' [[Nicolas Trigault]] (1577–1628) publishes in [[Augsburg]] the diaries of the [[Jesuits|Jesuit]] [[Matteo Ricci]] (1552–1610), which explain the game of weiqi; the book goes through at least 16 editions in European languages.<ref name=":11" />
* '''17th–18th century''' The philosopher [[Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz|Leibniz]] (1646–1716) publishes an article on Go with a picture of a game, saying he does not know all the rules.<ref name=":11" /><ref name=":11" />
* '''1694''' [[Thomas Hyde]] (1636–1703) writes the first detailed description of Go in a European language, in his treatise on oriental board games.<ref name=":11" /><ref name=":11" />
* '''1872''' The first recorded game of Go in Europe is played between [[Kido Takayoshi]] (1833–1877) and [[Ōkubo Toshimichi]] (1830–1878) of the [[Iwakura Mission]] (1871–1873).
* '''1880''' [[Oskar Korschelt]] (1853–1940), who learned the game in Japan from Murase Shūho, publishes ''Das Go Spiel'': the first systematic description in a Western language.<ref name="go5" /><ref name=":11" />
* '''1905–1908''' [[Edward Lasker]] (1885–1981) learns Go in Berlin (1905) and later founds the New York Go Club; in 1908 Arthur Smith publishes the first Go book in English, ''The Game of Go''.<ref name="agahbk95" /><ref name=":11" />
* '''1934–1937''' Lasker's ''Go and Go-moku'' (1934) helps popularize the game in the US; the [[American Go Association]] is formed in 1935 and the German Go Association in 1937.<ref name="agahbk95" /><ref name=":11" />
* '''1978''' [[Manfred Wimmer]] (1944–1995) becomes the first Westerner to receive a professional certificate from an East Asian Go association ([[Kansai Ki-in]]); James Kerwin is the first at the [[Nihon Ki-in]].<ref name="go6" /><ref name=":11" />
* '''1996''' Astronauts [[Daniel T. Barry|Daniel Barry]] (born 1953) and [[Koichi Wakata]] (born 1963) become the first people to play Go in space.{{sfn|Peng|Hall|1996}}
* '''2000''' The American [[Michael Redmond (Go player)|Michael Redmond]] (born 1963) is the first Westerner to reach 9 dan.<ref name="go7" /><ref name=":11" />
* '''2011–2014''' The [[American Go Association]] signs an agreement with the [[Korea Baduk Association|Korean Baduk Association]] to promote its own professional system (2011); the [[European Go Federation]] creates its own (2014).<ref name=":11" />
}}

