* '''October 2015''' [[AlphaGo]], made by [[Google]], beats the European champion [[Fan Hui]] (2p) 5–0 in even games: the first time a program beats a professional.<ref name=":11" />
* '''2016''' [[Google]]'s [[AlphaGo]] beats [[Lee Sedol]] (born 1983) 4–1 in Seoul, the first time an AI beats a top professional. The same year, [[Yuta Iyama|Iyama Yūta]] (born 1989) holds all seven Japanese major titles at once.<ref name=":11" />
* '''Late 2016 – early 2017''' A stronger [[AlphaGo]] ("Master") wins 60 games in a row, anonymously, online against top professionals.
* '''May 2017''' A new and much improved [[AlphaGo]] plays [[Ke Jie]] (born 1997) and wins all three games; Ke Jie says its play is "like a god of Go".<ref name=":11" />
* '''2019''' The open-source Go AI [[KataGo]] is created by David Wu.
* '''2019''' [[Lee Sedol]] announces his retirement, citing [[AlphaGo]] as "an entity that cannot be defeated".<ref name=":7" /><ref name=":8" /><ref name=":9" />
* '''2026''' [[Shin Jin-seo|Shin Jinseo]] (born 2000) defeats a new version of [[KataGo]] on a 2-stone handicap, coming back from a first-game defeat to win the next two.
}}

== Sensei's Library ==
{{#invoke:ViewSwitcher|switch |views=*Timeline=TimelineRail.outline; List=OutlineList.list; Table=OutlineTable.table
|id=gosl
|text=
* '''21 October 2000''' Two enthusiasts, Arno and Morten, start a [[wiki]] meant as an online repository of all Go knowledge: [[Sensei's Library]].<ref name=":11" />
* '''5 January 2001''' Sensei's Library opens to the public, announced on the Usenet group rec.games.go.<ref name=":11" />
* '''11 January 2004''' A French version, EncyGoPédie, is released.<ref name=":11" />
* '''11 November 2004''' The 10,000th page is added.<ref name=":11" />
* '''3 November 2007''' The first full-board (19×19) position search.<ref name=":11" />
* '''22 April 2009''' A customised board search is added.<ref name=":11" />
* '''28 May 2010''' The 20,000th page is created, by Dave Sigaty.<ref name=":11" />
}}

